display(df.head())
```

Expected output: `(768, 9)` — 768 records and 9 columns.

### Cell 3 — Separate input features and target variable

```python
X = df.drop("Outcome", axis=1)
y = df["Outcome"]

print("Input Features Shape:", X.shape)
print("Target Variable Shape:", y.shape)

print("\nInput Features:")
print(X.columns.tolist())
```

There are 8 input features and 1 target variable (`Outcome`).

### Cell 4 — Replace invalid zero values with NaN

Zero is invalid for Glucose, BloodPressure, SkinThickness, Insulin and BMI. However, zero pregnancies is valid, so we preserve it.

```python
zero_columns = [
    "Glucose",
    "BloodPressure",
    "SkinThickness",
    "Insulin",
    "BMI"
]

X[zero_columns] = X[zero_columns].replace(0, np.nan)

